			<div class="content" id="post-content-2227389">
									

<p>The second instance is me referencing a style of Native American myth which has the trickster let someone succeed (usually at something 'superhuman' or incredibly difficult), and then, delights in the failure of the next few. The really famous one that comes to mind is when Coyote told a tribe that he could raise their sons for them overnight. They were skeptical, but they gave him an orphan to try anyhow. He goes down to the frozen river, cuts a hole in the ice, wraps the baby up in cloth, and dunks him into the river. Leaves him there for a day, then comes back, tugs the rope, and out jumps a fully grown, handsome, smart warrior. All the women in the tribe rush to do the same thing, and they drown their babies.</p>
<p>This is sort of a really dark version, but nevertheless, it's the pattern I was seeking to emulate. Almost all of the instances of 'appearing to people' and 'causing trouble' are based on Native American trickster tales.</p>
<p>The only exception to that is Dr. Clef's entry, which I included to &quot;undercut&quot; the &quot;really wacky hijinks!&quot; that everyone expects Clef to have. Nope. Clef got his shirt stained. It's not a show down between two tricksters. Not a tale of Clef messing the guy up or outsmarting him. Nope. Clef&#8230; got his shirt stained.</p>

