			<div class="content" id="post-content-2227632">
									

<p>Fair reply. A few specific lines where I feel this could be tightened up, to give a clearer perspective of where I'm coming from:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">It is to be heavily guarded</span></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Usage of &quot;heavily&quot; here doesn't work for me. It's a relative word with no real context here and groks as not very clinical.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While basic amenities are allowed, these requests must be made through written statements, which are then carefully analyzed before being permitted.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is generally sort of weird and run-on-y. There are several spots where this multiple-comma sentence structure is used; if it's a stylistic choice then I guess we can agree to disagree but I'd say most of these instances would read with a more clinical tone if broken into smaller sentences. It feels unclear whether &quot;basic&quot; here is actual basic amenities or requests of more creature-comfort-y items. Saying the requests are &quot;carefully analyzed&quot; also seems unnecessary.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>people hearing them are simply convinced</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Simply&quot; is another case similar to &quot;heavily&quot; for me. Perhaps some in-universe argument about the document writer being under the skip's effect could be broached for this but I can't say that I'd buy it.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>You are right, it is a matter of taste. I didn't think the tone was <em>bad</em>, just off; if I thought it was outright bad I would have likely downvoted. Part of this is certainly just different style preferences. 2107, already linked above by Cyclopian, is a good example of my fairly dry tone. But for me, such as the mentioned examples, it's a matter of straying too far away from what I would quantify as clinical-sounding word choices and sentence structures.</p>
<p>I actually don't have much/any problem with the crossreferences or other aspects outside the normal format. I quite like the line usage separating sections, for example, and have thought about playing with that myself.</p>

							</div>

								
					
