					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=732274&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608714073" alt="thedeadlymoose" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=732274)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/thedeadlymoose" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(732274); return false;" >thedeadlymoose</a></span> <span class="odate time_1423380585 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Feb 2015 07:29</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2227836">
									

<p>We do need to use use 'style' and 'tone' to mean what they mean in the real world; otherwise the linguistic drift allows them to mean essentially anything the user wishes, which is confusing for writers who then have to decipher that feedback.</p>
<p>It's like the drift with our accidental redefinition of 'hook' to mean 'literally anything interesting or entertaining in a piece'. The actual meaning of 'hook' is 'something in the beginning of the piece that fascinates the reader and 'hooks' them in'.</p>
<p>I've seen endless confusion and hapless flailing on what people mean by &quot;tone&quot; or &quot;style&quot; or &quot;hook&quot; or all sorts of other words. That's what motivates me to point out the error here.</p>
<p>Tone is the work's attitude towards the subject and the audience. This is the meaning of the word.</p>
<p>Why is it important to talk about it here? Because this is <em>precisely</em> the kind of SCP that ends up getting the shaft because it's different from what we're used to. This page is absolute <em>bait</em> for taste-based critique framed as non-taste-based.</p>
<p>In this case, it won't get the shaft because Troy's an admin and a top writer. Another writer might not be so lucky.</p>
<p>Also, this is more aimed at others reading this page. We already know what you mean because you <em>did</em> clarify, so you're cool.</p>
<p>That said&#8230; at least one of those sentences is reasonable confusion. &quot;It is to be heavily guarded&quot; would definitely be bad tone by itself. As it stands, &quot;It is to be heavily guarded, with no fewer than eight regularly rotating guards and a constantly shifting staff&quot; is seriously awkward and should probably just be rephrased.</p>
<p>I probably shouldn't have been so picky when you called that particular sentence &quot;bad tone&quot; &#8212; to be honest, that's mostly me being a pedant.</p>
<p>EDIT: Also, a side note. The &quot;tone&quot; of this SCP <em>does</em> include the implied winks to the audience &#8212; unusual for a modern SCP article. So yeah there's definitely room to find the tone of the SCP offputting, even if not <em>incorrect.</em></p>

